taking another look at a general  election contest heading in towards uh  november of course we're looking at the  clark county district attorney race and  tim treffinger is here running for  district attorney as a republican tim it  is good to see you sir thank you it's a  pleasure to be here let's just get into  why you're running in the first place  against steve wolson who's been in  wolfson has been an incumbent for a long  time as you know  why jump into the race well part of it's  i believe in balance and i believe in  equality on both sides and the da's race  historically at least for the past  couple of cycles have just been a  democratic primary and when i saw that  no one was going to jump in on the  conservative side i felt it was  important to have those voices  recognized and that's why i'm in this  race what is it about mr wolfson's  performance as  as prosecutor in clark county that  concerns you that you think you would do  differently if you were elected into  office sure i think mainly it comes down  to a matter of leadership i mean he is  not going into court and prosecuting  cases he has taken a very administrative  role and i think because of that  disconnect between him and his deputies  not all of his policies are being  enforced they're not being done  consistently  there's a lot of great attorneys in that  office there's just a lack of leadership  and involvement in day-to-day running in  the court system you have said that you  will if you're elected pick up a case  from time to time to prosecute if you're  elected as a district attorney  what cases would you prosecute whatever  cases the office needed help with i  don't plan on just taking the high end  cases i think you need to pick up and  support your deputies even if you're  second chairing some cases anything to  do to support the office that way you're  plugged in with your deputies you know  what's going on day to day coming out of  your office maybe just a little more  specifically just to backtrack a little  bit of of you talk about the lack of  leadership at the district attorney's  office how has that manifested itself in  your point of view  in prosecutions in criminal justice and  just how the wheels of of the  prosecutor's office are moving sure it's  it's a matter of consistency i think  more than anything else you should be  able to walk into a courtroom similar  people facing similar charges and kind  of know what to expect but especially  recently and there's been many stories  about people who they believe should  have been charged maybe weren't because  they were financially involved or  friends of steve's or whoever  it needs to be consistent no matter who  you are whether you're a citizen a judge  a cop all the laws need to be enforced  efficiently and equally and that's just  not happening now you're making uh  you're addressing something that ozzy  fumo who ran against steve in the in the  democratic primary mentioned as well  sort of an allegation of favoritism  something that steve himself has  adamantly denied do you believe that the  prosecutor's office place favorites  i believe they shouldn't i haven't seen  enough evidence to firmly point a finger  and say he's playing favorites what i  can say is it's not happening consistent  consistently and whatever the reason for  that is i think it's inappropriate  another issue you've brought up is  family court and  corruption in family court what are some  examples that you think are happening  here in clark county well we have a lot  of issues in family court there's a lot  of inconsistency there too depending on  what department you're walking into it's  unclear how the law is going to be  applied we have contempt charges coming  up where people are not afforded the  right to counsel and are thrown in jail  for a period of time there's a lot of  different things there that i think can  either be handled better and if there  are people playing favorites or doing  corrupt things in family court that  should be investigated and that should  be prosecuted perhaps one thing maybe  viewers at home should note is you know  they may not know who you are tell me  about your background in law and your  experience in prosecution sure i came  out of law school from university of new  hampshire graduated with the jd and llm  in international criminal law and social  justice  came out to nevada out to virginia city  of all places story county where i spent  about a year  finishing my externship from there i  went into the nevada attorney general's  office where i prosecuted sex crimes  domestic violence crimes against women  for three counties  and then went into private practice and  i've been there ever since right now i  focus mainly on criminal defense and  family law  part of your past is your own experience  with with criminal prosecution you  entered a plea deal on heroin charges  back in 2015. maybe explain to the to  the viewers what happened back then and  maybe how that experience has  changed  you and maybe changed your your practice  and what you how you perceive the  justice system sure when i came out of  law school i expected to be a prosecutor  my entire career so being on the other  side of that table was certainly a  surreal experience  i went through the diversion program an  ex of mine developed a drug issue i was  trying to get her clean unfortunately in  the midst of trying to get her clean she  was selling drugs out of the house while  i wasn't home  police come and raid the house i end up  in the system myself i did i did do a  plea agreement for a possession charge i  went through the diversion program which  i am a huge proponent of that gives  people second chances who have never  been in trouble before or first-time  offenders  had my case dismissed but i think it  gives me an appreciation of both sides  of the table not only have i been a  prosecutor and a criminal defense  attorney i've been a defendant i  understand what that process is like i  understand the benefits of those  programs and giving people certain  people chances because probation in my  opinion isn't hard if you follow the  rules and i think everybody deserves a  second chance but you know if you can't  work within that system the prison is  always there for you so i am hard on  crime i just believe in diversion  programs  you so you mentioned how the your  experience uh with these charges you  learned that side of the diversion  program and probation and so forth were  there other things that you learned  about the prosecution standpoint that  you think maybe could be brought to  clark county absolutely i think my case  in particular  i was made an example of because i was a  prosecutor in that county  i think that most other people in my  situation would have been offered a  lighter deal than what i had to go  through and i think that just shows  favoritism isn't appropriate at any  level everybody should be treated the  same a police officer who's prosecuted  for domestic violence for example  shouldn't face harsher penalties than  the person on the street everybody  should be treated equally  you talked about the diversion programs  and so forth uh there's some specialty  courts in in clark county we've got the  drug courts veterans courts for example  should those continue do you think those  are successes yes veterans court in  particular i think is incredibly  important those people go through things  and experience trauma that most of us  everyday people have never been in the  military would never understand i think  that that court in particular is very  important drug court is incredibly  important drugs are wreaking havoc on  this county it's part of the reason that  our crime rate is so high and trying to  get people clean so that they don't keep  going back through the system over and  over again is an incredibly important  thing i think those programs could be  expanded without going too much into  taxpayer pockets and i believe that  everybody should have access to those  programs right now the da's office is  kind of gatekeeping everyone should be  able to apply it shouldn't be which  prosecutor you get decides what program  you can go into  another issue in nevada for years has  been the issue of the death penalty uh  the incumbent mr wolfson says he kind of  likes to have it out there just in case  essentially that  that's kind of why it's there  um  what's your stance on the death penalty  i am pro-death penalty but i believe  it's a specific tool for a specific  purpose it's for the worst of the worst  cases i don't think anyone here  or at least most people wouldn't argue  that the the october festival shooter  was not enough had he survived that  would have been an appropriate death  penalty case uh i don't believe it's for  every case i do believe it needs to be  you know instituted in an even and equal  manner and only for the worst of the  worst cases but i am pro-death penalty  that on that issue the issue of of the  specialty courts the issue of you know  other aspects family courts and so forth  a lot of the things you're describing  don't sound too dissimilar from the way  things are now just help me understand  the difference between you and your  opponent when people go into the ballot  box how are you different how would your  uh running the da's office be different  than mr wolfson i'm going to bring  equality and consistency if you if  you're able to apply for a specialty  corps program it shouldn't matter what  prosecutor in front of like i said that  should be available to everybody let the  judges decide who they allow into those  programs consistency of charging i'm  going to make sure the charges are  brought where it's appropriated whether  it's favoritism or just a negligence  whatever you want to call it because of  these charging decisions that type of  stuff is going to stop i'm also not for  decriminalizing shoplifting and things  that are causing damage to our small  businesses i believe i have a much  tougher stance on crime than steve does  and while we do agree on certain things  like the death penalty i still don't  agree with how consistently he applies  the death penalty he does use it in  limited cases but i don't always agree  that they're the correct cases or that  it's applied evenly  it's very interesting race and tim it's  good for you to come around and talk  with us about your entrance in as a  republican for the clark county district  attorney's office appreciate the time  thank you for having me running for  clark county district attorney

